\description{
\code{forceatlas2()} computes a ForceAtlas2 layout and stores it in the graph:
it returns the graph it was given with two node columns (by default \code{x} and
\code{y}) added. It is meant for \link[tidygraph:tbl_graph]{tidygraph::tbl_graph} objects and pipelines,
and works the same way on plain igraph graphs (the coordinates are then
stored as vertex attributes).

\code{layout_forceatlas2()} runs the same algorithm and returns the coordinates
as a two-column matrix, like the \verb{layout_*()} functions of igraph.

Both can be used with ggraph: either plot the stored coordinates with
\code{ggraph(graph, layout = "manual", x = x, y = y)}, or let ggraph compute the
layout with \code{ggraph(graph, layout = forceatlas2)} (further arguments such
as \code{linlog = TRUE} are passed on).
}
\details{
ForceAtlas2 is a force-directed layout: nodes repulse each other like
charged particles while edges attract the nodes they connect like springs.
Its particularity is that the repulsion between two nodes is proportional
to the product of their degrees (plus one), which pulls poorly connected
nodes close to the hubs they are attached to and makes communities stand
out.

This is an implementation in C of the algorithm described in Jacomy et al.
(2014). The forces, the adaptive speed and the default values are those of
the reference implementation distributed with Gephi, and the arguments map
to Gephi's settings as follows:\tabular{ll}{
   \strong{Gephi} \tab \strong{forceatlas2r} \cr
   Scaling \tab \code{scaling_ratio} \cr
   Gravity \tab \code{gravity} \cr
   Stronger Gravity \tab \code{strong_gravity} \cr
   LinLog mode \tab \code{linlog} \cr
   Dissuade Hubs \tab \code{dissuade_hubs} \cr
   Prevent Overlap \tab \code{prevent_overlap} \cr
   Edge Weight Influence \tab \code{edge_weight_influence} \cr
   Normalize edge weights \tab \code{normalize_weights} \cr
   Inverted edge weights \tab \code{invert_weights} \cr
   Tolerance (speed) \tab \code{jitter_tolerance} \cr
   Approximate Repulsion \tab \code{barnes_hut} \cr
   Approximation \tab \code{theta} \cr
   Threads number \tab \code{threads} \cr
}


Two things differ from Gephi. Gephi runs until the user stops it, whereas
here the number of \code{iterations} is fixed in advance. And with the
Barnes-Hut approximation, Gephi counts the repulsion between nearby nodes
twice; this implementation counts it once, so that the approximation
converges to the exact computation when \code{theta} goes to zero.

The mass of a node is its degree plus one, counting every edge (edge
weights and directions are ignored for the masses, multiple edges count
several times). Edge directions only matter when \code{dissuade_hubs = TRUE}.

The initial positions are drawn at random with R's random number generator
unless \code{init} is given: call \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} beforehand to obtain reproducible
layouts. For a given starting point, the result is deterministic and does
not depend on the number of threads.
}
